const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What makes mixed-use construction coordination different?",
    answer: "Residential, retail, office, parking, and public areas can have different systems, finish expectations, tenant decisions, inspection paths, operating hours, and occupancy goals while sharing the same structure, utilities, access, and site. The delivery plan must make those interfaces and responsibilities visible.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can phased occupancy be planned on a mixed-use project?",
    answer: "The team can map each intended release against life-safety conditions, permanent or temporary access, utilities, inspections, tenant readiness, shared-area completion, public separation, and authority requirements. The final sequence depends on the approved documents, jurisdiction, contracts, and owner priorities.",
  },
  {
    question: "When should construction planning begin for a mixed-use development?",
    answer: "Construction input is most useful while the team can still evaluate logistics, system interfaces, scope boundaries, procurement, phasing, budget assumptions, and constructability without disrupting completed design. Trinity Build Co.'s role and timing are defined by the selected delivery method and agreement.",
